<article class="env-lemma" id="06KT">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06KT">Lemma <span data-tag="06KT">9.4.7.22</span>.</a> Let $\kappa &lt; \lambda $ be infinite cardinals, let $(A, \leq )$ be a $\lambda $-directed partially ordered set, and suppose we are given a diagram of $\kappa $-small sets </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  (A, \leq ) \rightarrow \operatorname{Set}_{&lt; \kappa } \quad \quad \alpha \mapsto X_{\alpha }. \]
</div>
<p> Then the colimit $X = \varinjlim _{\alpha \in A} X_{\alpha }$ is also $\kappa $-small. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Assume otherwise. Then there exists a subset $Y \subseteq X$ having cardinality $\kappa $. For each $y \in Y$, we can choose an index $\alpha _ y \in A$ such that $y$ is the image of some element $\widetilde{y} \in X_{ \alpha _{y} }$. Since $Y$ is $\lambda $-small and $(A, \leq )$ is $\lambda $-directed, the set $\{  \alpha _{y}: y \in Y \} $ has an upper bound $\alpha $ in $A$. It follows that $Y$ is contained in the image of the map $X_{\alpha } \rightarrow X$, contradicting our assumption that $X_{\alpha }$ is $\kappa $-small. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</article>
